ARIA-1: An Adaptive Radiation-Immune Quadrant AI Accelerator with Hybrid Spike-Tensor Compute and Thermal-Aware RRAM Drift Management

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Date: June 2026

AI : ChatGpt, Claude, Deepseek 

Abstract

Deploying artificial intelligence at the orbital edge imposes a trilemma: no semiconductor architecture simultaneously satisfies real‑time inference performance, multi‑year radiation tolerance, and the power envelope of nanosatellites. This dissertation presents the ARIA‑1 accelerator, a heterogeneous system‑on‑chip that dynamically composes four compute domains to navigate this trade space. The architecture introduces (1) a quadrant‑domain SoC with a permanently active rad‑hard neuromorphic core, two RRAM‑based compute‑in‑memory engines of different total‑ionizing‑dose (TID) tolerances, and a novel Hybrid Spike‑Tensor Engine (HSTE) that natively executes both spiking neural network inference and integer tensor operations; (2) a flux‑event classifier that pairs on‑die PIN diode sensors with a lightweight leaky‑integrate‑and‑fire SNN, achieving sub‑millisecond classification of galactic cosmic ray transients, South Atlantic Anomaly passages, and solar particle events; (3) a thermal‑radiation coupled drift model for HfO₂ resistive RAM, derived from multiplicative interaction of radiation‑induced vacancy concentration and thermal‑activated migration, with a closed‑form expression for joint conductance variance; and (4) a thermal‑aware recalibration protocol with stuck‑at fault detection and redundant‑row remapping that maintains inference accuracy within 0.2% of baseline over a ten‑year mission. The domain controller is formalised as a continuous‑time Markov chain whose state‑transition logic is driven by the SNN classifier output and a thermal‑radiation drift estimator. Mathematical results include bounds on FORTRESS‑state occupancy, SNN detection latency, and drift‑variance magnification under combined stress. A cycle‑accurate simulation framework integrating neural network workloads, historical radiation traces, and device‑level RRAM variability models is constructed to validate all hypotheses. Projected effective throughput is 14–22 TOPS at 4.0 TOPS/W mean efficiency, with a five‑year system reliability of 83% under correlated failure assumptions.

