Chapter 1 — Introduction and Motivation

1.1 The Orbital AI Trilemma

Spacecraft at the orbital edge increasingly rely on on‑board artificial intelligence for tasks such as real‑time hyperspectral anomaly detection, synthetic‑aperture radar ship classification, and multi‑sensor fusion. The computational demands of modern deep neural networks conflict with two hard constraints:

1. Radiation environment. Low Earth orbit (LEO) and medium Earth orbit (MEO) expose electronics to a cumulative total ionising dose (TID) of 10–300 krad(Si) over five‑to‑ten‑year missions, together with single‑event effects (SEE) from trapped protons, galactic cosmic rays, and solar particle events (SEP).
2. Power budget. Nanosatellite platforms (3U–27U) typically provide 8–80 W of orbit‑average electrical power, of which the processing subsystem may consume 5–15 W.

Conventional solutions bifurcate the design space: radiation‑hardened processors (e.g., LEON4, GR740) offer reliability but deliver sub‑TOPS throughput; commercial off‑the‑shelf (COTS) accelerators achieve multi‑TOPS performance but require heavy triple‑modular redundancy that erodes power efficiency and still leaves corners vulnerable to common‑mode TID failure. No existing architecture adapts its compute fabric in response to the instantaneous radiation environment while maintaining high‑throughput inference across the full mission profile.

1.2 Environmental Self‑Awareness as a Design Principle

This dissertation adopts the thesis that a chip that can sense and classify its radiation environment in real time can dynamically re‑allocate its computational resources to maximise throughput subject to safety constraints. An adaptive architecture requires three capabilities:

· Fine‑grained flux measurement and event classification. Distinguishing between benign galactic cosmic ray showers, predictable South Atlantic Anomaly (SAA) passages, and potentially destructive SEP events is essential to avoid unnecessary performance gating.
· TID‑aware compute element selection. Different circuits have different dose‑hardness; a chip with multiple compute domains of graduated TID tolerance can selectively gate vulnerable domains during high‑flux intervals while maintaining mission continuity on rad‑hard cores.
· Thermal‑radiation coupled drift management. The conductance of resistive RAM (RRAM) weight cells drifts under combined thermal and radiation stress in a multiplicative fashion, not additively. An on‑line recalibration protocol must model this coupling to keep inference accuracy within specification over the mission lifetime.

1.3 Research Hypotheses

The architecture presented in this dissertation, ARIA‑1, is designed to test three hypotheses:

H1 (Quadrant Pareto optimality). A four‑domain architecture — consisting of a permanently active rad‑hard neuromorphic core (Domain A), two CIM engines of differing TID tolerance (Domains B and C), and a hybrid spike‑tensor engine (Domain D) — achieves a higher Pareto efficiency frontier (throughput vs. power vs. accuracy‑preservation under radiation) than any dual‑domain or homogeneous architecture across the LEO/MEO flux regime.

H2 (Coupled drift model fidelity). RRAM conductance drift under combined thermal and ionising radiation stress obeys a multiplicatively coupled stochastic model,
\Delta G_{ij}(t) = G_{ij}(0)\!\bigl[\epsilon_{\text{TID}}(D(t)) + \epsilon_T(T(t)) + \kappa\,\epsilon_{\text{TID}}(D(t))\cdot\epsilon_T(T(t))\bigr] + \xi_{ij}(t),


where $\kappa$ is a measurable coupling coefficient, and that a thermal‑aware recalibration protocol governed by the instantaneous joint drift rate can maintain inference accuracy within 0.2% of the commissioning baseline for ten years without ground intervention.

H3 (SNN‑augmented flux classification). A 512‑neuron leaky‑integrate‑and‑fire (LIF) spiking neural network, co‑located with on‑die PIN diode sensors and trained on historical proton flux time series, reduces the false‑positive rate of FORTRESS‑state transitions from >4% to below 0.8% while consuming less than 15 mW, thereby recovering throughput otherwise lost to unnecessary domain gating.

