Chapter 11 — System Evaluation and Hypothesis Validation

This chapter presents the simulation‑based evaluation of the ARIA‑1 v2 architecture against the three research hypotheses. We describe the simulation infrastructure, the benchmark workloads, and the radiation environment model, then report quantitative results that confirm H1, H2, and H3. All confidence intervals are computed from 100 Monte Carlo runs that sample process variation and radiation stochasticity.

11.1 Simulation Methodology

A cycle‑accurate SystemC model of the quadrant SoC is integrated with:

· Architectural simulator: models the four domains, the hierarchical NoC, the crossbar tiles with analogue‑to‑digital conversion delay, and the domain controller state machine.
· RRAM device model: calibrated to HfO₂ data (Pelke et al., 2025), including stochastic write variability, retention loss, and the coupled thermal‑radiation drift model of Chapter 3. Each crossbar cell is individually tracked.
· Radiation injector: replays 10‑year proton flux traces generated by SPENVIS for a sun‑synchronous 600 km orbit, with superimposed SEP events from the historical GOES/NOAA catalogue. TID accumulates per‑domain using dose‑depth curves; SEU injection follows the CREME96 model with per‑technology cross‑sections.
· Workload suite:
  · Hyperspectral anomaly detector (HSAD): 7‑layer CNN+SNN hybrid, 2.3 M parameters.
  · SAR ship classifier (SARSC): ResNet‑18 backbone, 11.2 M parameters, running on Domains B/C.
  · Temporal anomaly SNN: 3‑layer LIF, used as both mission inference and MFPI classifier training source.

Each simulation run executes a continuous 10‑year mission timeline, with domain controller transitions triggered by the simulated flux and MFPI classifier. MPNV‑2 recalibration events occur according to the adaptive cadence.

11.2 Hypothesis H1 — Quadrant Pareto Efficiency

We compare ARIA‑1 v2 against four alternative architectures:

· v1 dual‑domain: one neuromorphic core (32 k neurons) + one CIM engine, three‑state controller.
· All‑CIM homogeneous: 196 CIM tiles in 22 nm FD‑SOI with static TMR on peripherals.
· All‑neuromorphic: 1024 neuromorphic cores, no CIM.
· COTS‑with‑scrubbing: a 12 TOPS GPU‑class accelerator with external TMR and periodic memory scrubbing, modelled on existing NanoXplore solutions.

Each system is subjected to the same 10‑year radiation trace and runs the HSAD workload. The Pareto frontier is defined in the three‑dimensional space of (mean effective TOPS, mean power, accuracy degradation at 10 years).

