11.3 Hypothesis H2 — Coupled Drift Model Fidelity

The thermal‑radiation coupled drift model is evaluated by comparing the simulated conductance distribution of the Domain B crossbars after 10 years against predictions of the additive‑only model. The MPNV‑2 adaptive recalibration is enabled; the comparison metric is the mean‑squared error (MSE) between the actual weight matrix and the ideal target, normalised to the commissioning baseline.

Results (H2):

· With the coupled drift model and MPNV‑2 adaptive cadence, the normalised MSE remains within $[0.99, 1.01]$ of the baseline for the entire 10‑year period.
· The additive model underestimates the drift variance; under the same recalibration cadence, the additive model would allow the MSE to drift to 1.21 by year 7, exceeding the 0.2% accuracy degradation threshold.
· The coupling coefficient $\kappa = 0.38$ yields a predicted variance magnification factor of 1.73 at worst‑case thermal conditions; simulation confirms a factor of $1.68 \pm 0.04$ across the crossbar population, well within the ±0.07 uncertainty range.

The number of recalibration events is reduced by 38% compared to a fixed orbital‑period cadence, confirming the endurance benefit.

Conclusion for H2: Supported. The coupled model accurately captures the drift dynamics, and the thermal‑aware recalibration maintains inference accuracy within 0.2% for 10 years.

