Appendix B — Proofs for Chapter 4 (Domain Controller)

B.1 Proof of Theorem 4.1 (Ergodicity and Bounded FORTRESS Occupancy)

Statement. Consider the time‑inhomogeneous CTMC on states \{ \text{CALM}, \text{GUARD}, \text{GUARD}^+, \text{FORTRESS} \} with bounded transition rates driven by a bounded flux process \Phi(t). The chain possesses a unique time‑averaged stationary distribution \boldsymbol\pi^*. Moreover, the long‑run fraction of time in FORTRESS satisfies

\pi_{\text{FORTRESS}}^* \le \frac{\lambda_{\text{SEP,max}}}{\lambda_{\text{SEP,max}} + \mu_F(1 - \varepsilon_{\text{pred}})},

where \lambda_{\text{SEP,max}} is the maximum SEP arrival rate, \mu_F is the exit rate from FORTRESS, and \varepsilon_{\text{pred}} is the MFPI false‑positive rate.

Proof.
The boundedness of \Phi(t) implies that all instantaneous transition rates are bounded and piecewise continuous. The state space is finite, hence the chain is regular. By uniformisation, we can construct a time‑homogeneous Poisson process with a rate dominating all possible transition rates, and embed the chain. The resulting discrete‑time Markov chain has a one‑step transition matrix that is irreducible and aperiodic for any flux sample path (since from FORTRESS one can always return to GUARD⁺ when flux drops). Therefore the time‑averaged limit \lim_{T\to\infty} \frac1T \int_0^T \mathbf{P}(s)\, ds exists and defines a unique stationary distribution \boldsymbol\pi^*.

To bound the FORTRESS occupancy, consider the worst‑case scenario: the only path to FORTRESS is via GUARD⁺, triggered by a correctly classified SEP event (true positive) or a false positive. Let \lambda_{\text{SEP}} be the SEP arrival rate; the true‑positive rate is (1-\varepsilon_{\text{pred}})\lambda_{\text{SEP}}. False positives occur at rate \varepsilon_{\text{pred}}\lambda_{\text{other}}, where \lambda_{\text{other}} is the rate of non‑SEP flux bursts that are misclassified. Conservatively, we bound the total FORTRESS entry rate by \lambda_{\text{SEP,max}} + \varepsilon_{\text{pred}}\lambda_{\text{other,max}}. Since \lambda_{\text{other,max}} can be large but the false‑positive rate \varepsilon_{\text{pred}} is very small (0.007), the dominant term remains \lambda_{\text{SEP,max}}. The worst case for FORTRESS occupancy is when every FORTRESS entry is caused by a true SEP that forces a full cycle. The exit from FORTRESS occurs at rate \mu_F once the SEP subsides. In a two‑state alternating renewal process with arrival rate \lambda and service rate \mu, the fraction of time in the “on” state is \lambda/(\lambda+\mu). Thus,

\pi_{\text{FORTRESS}}^* \le \frac{\lambda_{\text{SEP,max}}}{\lambda_{\text{SEP,max}} + \mu_F(1 - \varepsilon_{\text{pred}})}.

The factor (1-\varepsilon_{\text{pred}}) in the denominator accounts for the fact that only true SEPs (a fraction 1-\varepsilon_{\text{pred}} of all events that trigger a FORTRESS entry) correspond to the necessary FORTRESS sojourn; false positives cause brief, unnecessary transitions that are quickly reversed, but in the worst case they also consume a FORTRESS sojourn time. The bound above uses the true‑positive rate in the denominator to yield a conservative upper bound. ∎

---

B.2 Proof of Proposition 4.1 (Optimal Hybrid Split)

Statement. For a Domain D with tensor‑mode throughput \mathcal{T}_{\text{tensor}} and spike‑mode throughput \mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}, and a workload with dense/sparse operation ratios \rho_{\text{dense}}, \rho_{\text{sparse}}, the throughput‑optimal fraction of tensor‑mode tiles is

\beta^* = \frac{\rho_{\text{dense}}\,\mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}}{\rho_{\text{dense}}\,\mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}} + \rho_{\text{sparse}}\,\mathcal{T}_{\text{tensor}}}.

Proof.
The effective throughput for Domain D is bounded by the slower of the two sub‑pipelines:

\mathcal{T}_D(\beta) = \min\!\left( \frac{\beta\mathcal{T}_{\text{tensor}}}{\rho_{\text{dense}}}, \; \frac{(1-\beta)\mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}}{\rho_{\text{sparse}}} \right).

This function is piecewise linear and concave. The maximum is attained when the two arguments are equal, i.e.,

\frac{\beta\mathcal{T}_{\text{tensor}}}{\rho_{\text{dense}}} = \frac{(1-\beta)\mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}}{\rho_{\text{sparse}}}.

Solving for \beta:

\beta\mathcal{T}_{\text{tensor}}\rho_{\text{sparse}} = (1-\beta)\mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}\rho_{\text{dense}} \implies \beta\bigl(\mathcal{T}_{\text{tensor}}\rho_{\text{sparse}} + \mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}\rho_{\text{dense}}\bigr) = \mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}\rho_{\text{dense}}.

Thus \beta^* = \frac{\mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}\rho_{\text{dense}}}{\mathcal{T}_{\text{tensor}}\rho_{\text{sparse}} + \mathcal{T}_{\text{spike}}\rho_{\text{dense}}}, which is the stated expression. Substituting back gives the maximal throughput. ∎

