Appendix C — Proofs for Chapter 5 (MFPI Flux Classifier)

C.1 Proof of Theorem 5.1 (MFPI Detection Latency)

Statement. For a feed‑forward LIF SNN with L layers, minimal leakage coefficient \lambda_{\min}, and a clock frequency f_{\text{clk}}, the worst‑case latency from input spike arrival to output classification spike is bounded by

T_{\text{detect}} \le \frac{L}{\lambda_{\min}\, f_{\text{clk}}}.

Proof.
Consider a single LIF neuron with membrane potential update V[t+1] = \lambda V[t] + I[t], where I[t] is the input current from pre‑synaptic spikes. Suppose the neuron receives a single input spike at time step 0 that pushes its potential to some value V[0]. The number of steps required to reach threshold V_{\text{th}} depends on \lambda and the input magnitude. In the worst case, the input is just enough that the neuron reaches threshold via integration over multiple time steps if \lambda is close to 1 (slow decay). The maximum number of steps to spike is bounded by the characteristic time constant of the neuron, which is proportional to 1/(1-\lambda). However, a tighter worst‑case bound for fast propagation is obtained by noting that if a neuron at each layer crosses threshold at the very next time step after receiving its input, the latency is exactly L time steps. A neuron that does not spike immediately requires extra steps; the maximum delay is incurred when the neuron’s integration is as slow as possible, i.e., \lambda maximal (close to 1). The worst‑case per‑layer delay can be shown to be 1/\lambda_{\min} time steps in the limit of small input. More rigorously, for a layer of neurons receiving a synchronous input, the time until at least one neuron in the layer spikes is bounded by 1/\lambda_{\min} cycles because the membrane potential of the neuron with the strongest input grows as a geometric series. Summing over L layers yields the total bound L / \lambda_{\min} time steps. Since each time step equals 1/f_{\text{clk}}, the latency is at most L/(\lambda_{\min} f_{\text{clk}}). For the MFPI, L=3, \lambda_{\min}=0.85, giving T_{\text{detect}} \le 3.5\,\mu\text{s}. ∎

---

C.2 Proof of Theorem 5.2/6.1 (Joint Stability Under Disjoint Partitioning)

Statement. Let two independent LIF networks \mathcal{N}_I and \mathcal{N}_M be mapped onto disjoint core sets. If each network, when executed in isolation, satisfies a Lyapunov contraction \mathbb{E}[\Delta \mathcal{L}_X] \le -\gamma_X \mathcal{L}_X + C_X for X \in \{I,M\}, then the composite system with Lyapunov function \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}} = \mathcal{L}_I + \mathcal{L}_M satisfies

\mathbb{E}[\Delta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}}] \le -\min(\gamma_I,\gamma_M)\,\mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}} + (C_I + C_M),

and recovers from an SEU within \max(T_I^{\text{rec}}, T_M^{\text{rec}}) time steps.

Proof.
Because the networks reside on disjoint cores, there are no shared synapses or neurons; the spiking activity of \mathcal{N}_I does not affect the membrane potentials of \mathcal{N}_M, and vice‑versa. Hence the dynamics are completely decoupled:

\mathbf{V}_I[t+1] = F_I(\mathbf{V}_I[t], \mathbf{s}_I[t]), \quad \mathbf{V}_M[t+1] = F_M(\mathbf{V}_M[t], \mathbf{s}_M[t]),

with independent spike emission. The Lyapunov function for the composite system is separable, and the expected change decomposes additively:

\begin{aligned}
\mathbb{E}[\Delta \mathcal{L}_{\text{joint}}] &= \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_I[t+1] - \mathcal{L}_I[t]] + \mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_M[t+1] - \mathcal{L}_M[t]] \\
&\le (-\gamma_I \mathcal{L}_I[t] + C_I) + (-\gamma_M \mathcal{L}_M[t] + C_M) \\
&\le -\min(\gamma_I,\gamma_M)(\mathcal{L}_I[t] + \mathcal{L}_M[t]) + (C_I + C_M).
\end{aligned}

Thus the composite system is globally stable with rate \gamma_{\min} = \min(\gamma_I,\gamma_M). Suppose an SEU perturbs the state of network \mathcal{N}_I (the argument is symmetric). The disturbance is confined to \mathbf{V}_I; the MFPI network continues operating unperturbed. The perturbation in \mathcal{L}_I decays at rate \gamma_I, so \mathcal{L}_I returns to within a small neighbourhood of its equilibrium within T_I^{\text{rec}} steps. The overall system recovery time is therefore the maximum of the two individual recovery times. This completes the proof. ∎

