Appendix D — Proofs for Chapter 7 (MPNV Convergence Under Remapping)

D.1 Proof of Theorem 7.1 (MPNV Convergence Under Remapping)

Statement. If the spare rows/columns in a crossbar tile are populated with cells from the same conductance distribution as the active cells, and programmed using the identical MPNV iterative write‑verify procedure, the root‑mean‑square error of the logical matrix‑vector product after stuck‑at‑fault remapping is bounded by the same constant 0.072\,\Delta G_{\text{window}} as in a defect‑free tile.

Proof.
Let \mathbf{G}_k^{\text{phys}} be the physical conductance matrix after remapping, and \mathbf{G}_k the target logical matrix. The remapping is a permutation \pi of rows and columns such that \mathbf{G}_k^{\text{phys}} = \mathbf{P}_{\text{row}} \mathbf{G}_k^{\text{spare}} \mathbf{P}_{\text{col}}, where \mathbf{G}_k^{\text{spare}} is the matrix obtained by replacing stuck‑at cells with freshly programmed spare cells. The logical‑to‑physical mapping ensures that for any logical address (i,j), the physical cell used is either the original (if healthy) or a spare. Because the spare cells undergo the identical MPNV programming process, their final conductance errors \delta g are drawn from the same distribution as the errors of healthy cells after the same number of write‑verify cycles. Let \mathbf{E} be the error matrix of all cells (both original and spare). Then \mathbf{G}_k^{\text{phys}} = \mathbf{G}_k + \mathbf{E}. The key observation is that the set of error entries that contribute to any logical dot product is a subset of the full error matrix, with each entry statistically identical to the baseline distribution. The mean‑squared error of the dot product \mathbf{y} = \mathbf{G}_k^{\text{phys}} \mathbf{x} against the target \mathbf{G}_k \mathbf{x} is

\mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{y} - \mathbf{G}_k\mathbf{x}\|^2] = \mathbb{E}[\|\mathbf{E}\mathbf{x}\|^2].

Since the rows (and columns) of \mathbf{E} are independent and identically distributed (after remapping), the analysis from the defect‑free case applies unchanged. The worst‑case RMS error per output element was shown in prior work to be at most 0.072\,\Delta G_{\text{window}}\|\mathbf{x}\|. Thus the bound holds for the remapped tile as well. ∎

