Appendix F — Proofs for Chapter 9 (MPNV‑2 Convergence with Stuck‑At Remapping)

F.1 Proof of Theorem 9.1 (MPNV‑2 Convergence with Stuck‑At Remapping)

Statement. Under the same spare‑cell conditions as in Theorem 7.1, the MPNV‑2 algorithm that detects stuck‑at faults and remaps them to spares preserves the RMS error convergence bound of 0.072\,\Delta G_{\text{window}}.

Proof.
The MPNV‑2 algorithm consists of two concurrent processes: (a) iterative write‑verify on all cells identified as non‑stuck, and (b) upon detection of a stuck‑at cell, marking it, selecting a spare, and programming that spare using the same iterative loop. Process (b) interrupts the recalibration only momentarily; after the spare is programmed, the tile’s logical weight matrix is again fully represented by a set of cells that have all been programmed with the same algorithm. The convergence proof of the MPNV algorithm relies on the fact that each cell’s conductance error evolves according to a contraction mapping in expectation, independent of the other cells, and that the per‑step error reduction is uniform across the tile. Introducing a spare cell that starts from a reset state and is then subjected to the identical pulse sequence does not alter the contraction property; the spare simply joins the active set after its programming completes. Therefore, the global RMS error remains bounded by the same constant. ∎

