Appendix G — Proofs for Chapter 10 (Patch Size Upper Bound)

G.1 Proof of Theorem 10.1 (Patch Size Upper Bound)

Statement. For a neural‑network layer with W weights, of which a fraction p are updated, an OrbBytecode v2 patch requires at most

S_{\text{patch}} \le H + 34\,pW \text{ bits},

where H is a fixed header size (32 bytes = 256 bits).

Proof.
Each updated weight is encoded as a triple (16‑bit logical row, 16‑bit column, 16‑bit target conductance) plus a 2‑bit domain tag. Thus each updated weight consumes 16+16+16+2 = 50 bits. However, because the row and column can be combined into a single 16‑bit linear address when the layer dimensions are known, we actually use a 16‑bit address and a 16‑bit value, giving 34 bits per weight (including the 2‑bit domain tag packed with the address). More formally, the patch format is:

· Header H: 32 bytes (layer ID, sequence number, ECC).
· For each of the pW updated weights: 2 bytes logical address, 2 bytes conductance value, and a 2‑bit domain identifier shared across a group of four weights, amortising to 0.5 bits per weight. Thus total per weight = 16 + 16 + 0.5 = 32.5 bits. Rounding up yields 34 bits for simplicity (the extra bit can be used for alignment). Hence, the total payload is H + 34 pW bits. This is clearly an upper bound because any compression would only reduce the size. ∎

